    "于夜色深处，一只可怖的怪物在冰湖城展开了它的狩猎。为了调查“卡戎狩猎”这一惨案的真相，夜航者小队接下了卫戍部队下级军官——黎瑟的委托……": "In the dead of night, a grotesque creature stalked the streets of Icelake. Seeking the truth behind the tragedy known as the Charon Hunt, the Noctoyagers accepted a request from Rhythm, a junior officer in the Icelake Escort...",
    "于绽放的刹那凝结成永恒。": "Eternity, captured at the moment of bloom.",
    "余观古昔圣贤所以教人为学之意，莫非使其穷究义理、博览古今，以拓心胸之格局，开眼界之高远；而后明辨是非、辩证观物，洞察世事之理，非徒然务记览、工词章，以钓声名、取利禄而已。\n\n今人之为学者，苦久矣。前人所教之法，具多于典籍之内，不落世事。有志之士，固当熟读深思而问辨之。苟知天地之大、事理之繁，求以客观之姿、辩证之思观照万物，则夫立身行事之准则，岂待他人设之而后有所持循哉！\n\n余之所撰，固然未能遍导诸生，而其为法，又未必合拓智启思之本意也。故今不复以施于此堂，而特取凡圣贤所以教人为学之大端，条例如左，而揭之相间。诸君相与讲明遵守，而责之于身焉。则夫思虑云为之际，其所以审思明辨、格物致知者，必有严于彼者矣。实为立身行世、修德向善之准则根本。\n\n惟守此纲纪，而后观照万物，方能生新识，拓新途。": "In considering what the sages of old intended when they taught learning, I take it to be this: to press inquiry to its limits, and to read widely in the old and the new, so as to broaden the mind and enlarge one's view. Only then may a person judge right from wrong, weigh matters with clarity, and grasp the workings of the world — not merely memorise texts and polish fine phrases for the sake of fame, profit, and preferment.\n\nThose who study in our day have borne hardships for too long. Much of what the former worthies taught is recorded in the classics, yet remains detached from worldly affairs. Anyone with resolve ought to read deeply, reflect, and question — to examine and sift what one has learned. For if one recognises the vastness of Heaven and Earth and the complexity of affairs, and strives to look upon all things with objectivity and a mind trained to weigh matters with discernment, then the standards by which one stands in the world and conducts oneself are already within one's grasp. Why wait for others to hand them over before one can hold fast to them?\n\nAs for what I have written, it cannot guide every student, and the methods it proposes may not fully accord with the true aim of opening the mind and awakening thought. Therefore I shall no longer require it in this hall. Instead, I have selected the main principles by which the sages taught learning, set them forth as articles, and posted them here for all to see. Let all of you study them together, make their meaning clear, keep them, and hold yourselves to them. Then, in the midst of your thoughts and actions, your judgement will grow more exacting: you will deliberate with care, discern with clarity, and investigate things so as to extend your knowledge. These are, in truth, the roots of conduct in the world, and the foundations of cultivating virtue and turning towards the good.\n\nOnly by keeping to this guiding framework may one contemplate all things, and so bring forth new understanding and open new paths.",
